        <div id="rdR1Cards"></div>
        <div class="rd-viz" id="rdR1Viz"></div>
        <p class="rd-register">Historical, at this position &mdash; not a prediction. Raising cash for a bill or a deadline is a different question, and the record has no view on it.</p>
        <p class="rd-route-line"><a class="rd-route" href="/how-much-cash">How Much Cash? &rarr;</a></p>
        <div class="rd-sources"><strong>Sources.</strong> The shared channel-entry engine at its default rebuy rule &mdash; the first lower entry within two years, which is where <a href="/how-much-cash">How Much Cash?</a> sits with its own slider untouched. Matched on today&rsquo;s clamped position, 2014 onward, measured in coins. The win rate is the figure <a href="/wait-or-deploy-now">Wait, or Deploy Now?</a> prints as its headline for the same position. The median coins-back figure is the engine&rsquo;s own, <strong>before</strong> the share of stack sold and the tax rate are applied &mdash; How Much Cash applies both and prints a lower number at its defaults, which is the point of going there.</div>
      </article>


      <!-- ── B1 · the rebalancing bands ── -->
      <article class="rd-snack" id="snack-b1" data-intent="rebalance">
        <p class="rd-q">If you run bands, how far are they from triggering here?</p>
        <p class="rd-verdict" id="rdB1Verdict"></p>
        <div id="rdB1Cards"></div>
        <div class="rd-viz" id="rdB1Viz"></div>
        <p class="rd-register">Where price sits now against the tool&rsquo;s own standard settings &mdash; not a prediction, and not your bands unless you have set them there.</p>
        <p class="rd-route-line"><a class="rd-route" href="/disciplined-rebalancing">Disciplined Rebalancing &rarr;</a></p>
        <div class="rd-sources"><strong>Sources.</strong> <a href="/disciplined-rebalancing">Disciplined Rebalancing</a>&rsquo;s standard preset &mdash; sell at the 80th percentile of the historical price-to-trend ratio, rebuy at the 50th &mdash; computed from the same record that page ranks, and priced against today&rsquo;s trend. Move either slider there and these lines move with it. Nothing here reads or changes what you have set on that page.</div>
      </article>

      <!-- ── P1 · the retirement threshold (§5 pattern) ── -->
      <article class="rd-snack" id="snack-p1" data-intent="retire">
        <p class="rd-q">What stack would your target need, read from here?</p>
        <p class="rd-verdict" id="rdP1Verdict"></p>
        <div id="rdP1Cards"></div>
        <div class="rd-viz" id="rdP1Viz"></div>
        <p class="rd-note" id="rdP1Basis"></p>
        <p class="rd-register">A conditional projection, not a forecast: each figure names the case it assumes, with the alternative beside it. <a href="#what-would-break-this">What would break this &rarr;</a></p>
        <p class="rd-route-line"><a class="rd-route" href="/bitcoin-escape-velocity">Bitcoin Escape Velocity &rarr;</a></p>
        <div class="rd-sources"><strong>Sources.</strong> The shared retirement engine&rsquo;s own threshold solver &mdash; the smallest stack whose real value stops falling for every year of the plan. <a href="/bitcoin-escape-velocity">Bitcoin Escape Velocity</a> is where it is published; the retirement flagship does not compute it. To reproduce these figures there, set the same year and income and switch the price basis to &ldquo;today&rsquo;s gap to trend persists&rdquo; for the first, or set the site-wide growth model to the floor case for the second. The horizon is fixed here at 30 years and is adjustable there.</div>
      </article>

      <!-- ── P2 · drawdowns from this zone ── -->
      <article class="rd-snack" id="snack-p2" data-intent="retire">
        <p class="rd-q">What have drawdowns from this zone looked like?</p>
        <p class="rd-verdict" id="rdP2Verdict"></p>
        <div id="rdP2Cards"></div>
        <div class="rd-viz" id="rdP2Viz"></div>
        <p class="rd-register">Historical, at this position &mdash; not a prediction. Shallow falls from here are close to arithmetic: an entry near the floor has less room beneath it than one near the trend line.</p>
        <p class="rd-route-line"><a class="rd-route" href="/the-bitcoin-retirement-stress-test">The Retirement Stress Test &rarr;</a></p>
        <div class="rd-sources"><strong>Sources.</strong> The shared channel-entry engine &mdash; drawdown frequency, median depth and the never-fell share over a two-year forward window, matched on today&rsquo;s clamped position, 2014 onward. A &ldquo;drawdown&rdquo; here is a fall of 20% or more, and the window is two years; both are the engine&rsquo;s own constants. <a href="/the-bitcoin-retirement-stress-test">The Stress Test</a> is a what-if tool for a fall of a size you choose, not the source of these figures and not where they are checked.</div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ WHAT IS NOT HERE YET (§2.6) ══════════ -->
  <section class="rd-section" id="not-here-yet">
    <h2>What is <em>not</em> here yet</h2>
    <div class="rd-roadmap">
      <p>Modules appear when an engine exists to compute them from and their figures reproduce on the page that owns them. These do not exist yet, and a module that guessed would be worse than no module. The item bearing on your chosen question is marked.</p>
      <ul>
        <li data-absent="dca"><strong>Whether to start a DCA now, on its own terms.</strong> <a href="/what-daily-conviction-bought">What Daily Conviction Bought</a> runs a DCA from a start date you choose, but nothing in it varies with where price sits in the channel. The ladder module above is the nearest honest read, not a substitute for one.</li>
        <li data-absent="rebalance"><strong>What rebalancing from this position has historically done.</strong> The bands module above is a now-read: it says where the lines sit today. Asking whether triggering here has paid needs the protocol run from each historical entry in the zone, which no engine does.</li>
        <li data-absent="retire"><strong>Sequence risk conditioned on this position.</strong> The threshold module asks what a plan needs; it does not ask what starting a withdrawal plan from <em>this</em> position has meant for the order returns arrived in. That needs the withdrawal loop run from each historical entry in the zone &mdash; a real piece of work, and the most valuable of these three.</li>
        <li data-absent="cash"><strong>Selling for a reason that is not timing.</strong> Need, risk reduction and sticking to a plan are legitimate and none of them is a mistake. The record has no view on any of them, and a module built from it would be answering a question nobody asked.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="rd-note">A map, growing &mdash; never a matrix, padded.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ WHAT WOULD BREAK THIS (§2.7) ══════════ -->
  <section class="rd-section" id="what-would-break-this">
    <h2>What would <em>break</em> this</h2>
    <p>This is the most model-dependent page on the site. Every figure above rests on one model, and there is no second method here quietly agreeing with the first &mdash; if the power law is not a real long-run structure, the position this page is organised around is not a position, and the visits it counts are not visits.</p>
    <p><strong>The floor is the model&rsquo;s own tripwire.</strong> <a href="/the-bitcoin-floor">The Bitcoin Floor</a> states the criterion in advance &mdash; how far below, for how long &mdash; and reports against it. That criterion is the one this page would fail with, and it is stated there rather than restated here so it has one home rather than two wordings.</p>
    <p><strong>The unsampled scenario is the important one.</strong> The record behind this page contains no instance of the floor giving way and staying gone, so nothing above is evidence that it cannot. The shared engine goes further and clamps sub-floor positions before matching entries at all, which means a sustained break is not merely rare in this data &mdash; it is structurally absent from it. An absence in a sample is not a property of the world, and breaks run in both directions.</p>
  </section>

